(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What happens to the ability to discriminate sound patterns when the auditory cortex, with its tonotopic map organization, is destroyed?","choices":["Only high-frequency discrimination is lost, low-frequency is unaffected","Sound pattern discrimination is unaffected","Discrimination of sound patterns is lost","Hearing sensitivity actually improves"],"answer":2,"exp":"The auditory cortex and its association areas are organized by tonotopic maps (high frequency at one end, low at the other); when the auditory cortex is destroyed, discrimination of sound patterns is lost even if basic sound detection may persist at lower levels. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How do basilar membrane fiber length and diameter change from the base of the cochlea to the helicotrema?","choices":["Fiber length and diameter both increase toward the helicotrema","Fiber length decreases and diameter increases toward the helicotrema","Both length and diameter stay constant","Fiber length increases and diameter decreases toward the helicotrema"],"answer":3,"exp":"From the base (near the oval window) to the helicotrema, basilar membrane fiber length increases while fiber diameter decreases, producing the roughly 100-fold decrease in stiffness that underlies the place principle of frequency detection. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Approximately how many times more outer hair cells are there compared to inner hair cells in the organ of Corti?","choices":["There are more inner hair cells than outer hair cells","10 times as many outer hair cells","3-4 times as many outer hair cells","Equal numbers of each"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are approximately 3-4 times as many outer hair cells as inner hair cells, even though inner hair cells transmit about 90% of auditory signals — outer hair cells are thought to modulate inner hair cell sensitivity. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How many of the 13 possible taste bud chemoreceptor types are dedicated to detecting sodium?","choices":["0","5","All 13","2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Of the 13 possible chemical receptor types found in taste buds, 2 are dedicated to sodium detection (alongside 2 for potassium, plus receptors for chloride, adenosine, inosine, 2 sweet, 2 bitter, glutamate, and hydrogen ion). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which bitter compound has a lower detection threshold — quinine or strychnine?","choices":["Strychnine, at 0.0001 mM, is far more sensitive (detectable at lower concentration) than quinine at 0.008 mM","Both have thresholds of exactly 0.001 mM, making them equally detectable at low concentrations","Neither compound activates bitter receptors; both instead stimulate only sour taste pathways","Quinine, detectable at 0.00001 mM, is far more sensitive than strychnine at 0.008 mM"],"answer":0,"exp":"Strychnine has a threshold of about 0.0001 mM, making it detectable at a much lower concentration (i.e., more sensitively perceived) than quinine, which has a threshold of about 0.008 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the approximate detection threshold for citric acid, producing the sour taste sensation?","choices":["20 mM","0.008 mM","2 mM","200 mM"],"answer":2,"exp":"The threshold for detecting citric acid (which produces sour taste via H+ ions) is approximately 2 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Approximately how many taste buds does the human tongue contain?","choices":["300-1,000","3,000-10,000","100,000-500,000","Over 1 million"],"answer":1,"exp":"The human tongue contains approximately 3,000 to 10,000 taste buds, with an average individual taste bud lifespan of about 10 days. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Where exactly are odorant-binding protein receptors located within the olfactory system?","choices":["Only in the olfactory bulb neurons","On the tectorial membrane","On the cilia of olfactory cells","On the tongue's fungiform papillae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Odorant-binding protein receptors are located on the cilia of olfactory cells within the olfactory membrane; binding of a chemical odorant to these receptors triggers the G-protein/cAMP transduction cascade. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Approximately what frequency is a typical human voice, which the attenuation reflex helps dampen?","choices":["~10,000 Hz","~100 Hz","~1000 Hz","~100,000 Hz"],"answer":2,"exp":"The attenuation reflex helps dampen low-frequency sounds such as one's own voice, approximately 1000 Hz, protecting the cochlea and reducing the perceived loudness of self-generated or others' speech. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why is it functionally important that the tympanic membrane's area is much larger than the oval window's area?","choices":["It only matters for very loud sounds above 100 decibels, providing essentially no benefit during ordinary conversational-level hearing","The large area-to-small-area ratio concentrates the force of airborne sound vibrations, amplifying the pressure delivered to the fluid-filled cochlea at the oval window","It has essentially no functional importance, since the round window alone provides all necessary impedance matching for the cochlea","It slows the transmission of sound by several milliseconds, protecting the delicate cochlea from sudden transient pressure spikes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because the tympanic membrane's area is about 17 times larger than the oval window's, the force collected over the larger membrane area is concentrated onto the much smaller oval window, amplifying pressure and helping overcome the impedance mismatch between air and cochlear fluid. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Do all five basic taste qualities (salty, sweet, sour, bitter, umami) use the identical transduction mechanism to depolarize taste cells?","choices":["Only sour taste causes depolarization via direct H+ channel blockade; the other four taste qualities rely on non-electrical transduction mechanisms","No taste quality causes true membrane depolarization; all five instead signal exclusively through direct synaptic vesicle release","Yes, all five taste qualities use an identical G-protein-coupled receptor pathway that strictly requires external calcium entry to function","No — salty, sweet, sour, and umami cause depolarization via different mechanisms, while bitter uniquely relies on internal Ca2+ release with no external calcium required"],"answer":3,"exp":"Salty, sweet, sour, and umami tastes each cause depolarization of their respective taste cells via distinct mechanisms, while bitter taste transduction is unique in causing an internal release of Ca2+ without requiring external calcium entry. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What structure encases and protects the cochlea?","choices":["Bone","Only skin","The tympanic membrane alone","Cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cochlea is encased in bone, providing rigid protection for its delicate fluid-filled structures. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What happens to heart rate when acetylcholine receptors are blocked with atropine?","choices":["Heart rate is completely unaffected, since atropine acts only on nicotinic receptors in the adrenal medulla","Atropine has no effect on the cardiovascular system, acting exclusively on smooth muscle within the gastrointestinal tract","Heart rate decreases further, since atropine potentiates rather than blocks the vagal slowing effect on the SA node","Heart rate increases, since atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors mediating the vagal slowing effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blocking acetylcholine (muscarinic) receptors with atropine removes the vagal restraint on the SA node, causing heart rate to increase (unmasking the SA node's faster intrinsic discharge rate). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"In a normal heart, from what general anatomic direction does the mean electrical vector of the partially depolarized heart typically point?","choices":["From base to apex (and top to bottom, back to front, right to left)","From left to right only (with no vertical or anteroposterior component)","From apex to base (and bottom to top, front to back)","The mean vector changes direction randomly with each individual heartbeat"],"answer":0,"exp":"In a normally depolarizing heart, the mean electrical vector typically points from base to apex (and correspondingly from top to bottom, back to front, and right to left), reflecting the normal sequence of ventricular depolarization. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does the septum and endocardium show a longer contraction duration compared to the outer ventricular surface?","choices":["The septum/endocardium is the first area to depolarize and the last to repolarize, giving it a longer action potential/contraction duration; the outer ventricular surface is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize","The outer ventricular surface is the first area to depolarize and the last to repolarize, giving it a longer contraction duration than the septum, which depolarizes last but repolarizes first","Depolarization and repolarization occur simultaneously everywhere in the ventricle at once, so contraction duration is identical throughout both the septum and the outer wall","The septum never depolarizes at all, remaining electrically silent throughout the entire cardiac cycle while only the outer ventricular wall depolarizes and contracts normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The septum and endocardium are the first areas to depolarize but the last to repolarize, giving them a longer contraction duration; conversely, the outer surface of the ventricles is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the functional purpose of the delay at the A-V node?","choices":["To speed up conduction to the ventricles, ensuring atrial and ventricular contraction occur simultaneously","To allow the atria to finish contracting and empty their contents into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins","The A-V node delay serves no functional purpose, existing only as a vestigial remnant of embryonic conduction tissue","To prevent the atria from ever contracting, redirecting all electrical activity directly to the ventricles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The A-V node delay (about 0.09 seconds, the largest component of AV conduction delay) allows the atria to finish contracting and empty their blood into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins, optimizing ventricular filling. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the overall purpose of the cardiac conduction system?","choices":["To produce the heart sounds heard on auscultation, generated directly by electrical current passing through cardiac tissue","To regulate blood pressure independent of the heart, acting through direct innervation of peripheral arteriolar smooth muscle","To generate the mechanical force of contraction directly, bypassing the need for excitation-contraction coupling in myocytes","To generate and rapidly distribute the electrical impulse that triggers coordinated atrial and then ventricular contraction, with an appropriate delay between the two"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cardiac conduction system (SA node, internodal pathways, A-V node, A-V bundle, and Purkinje fibers) generates and distributes the electrical impulse that triggers coordinated contraction — first of the atria, then (after an appropriate A-V nodal delay) of the ventricles. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the anatomic path that Purkinje fibers follow to reach ventricular muscle?","choices":["Directly from the SA node to the ventricular apex, bypassing the A-V node entirely","From the ventricular apex back up to the atria","Purkinje fibers do not connect to the A-V node at all","From the A-V node, through the A-V bundle, into the ventricles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Purkinje fibers lead from the A-V node, through the A-V bundle, into the ventricles, providing fast conduction (via many gap junctions at intercalated disks) to rapidly distribute the impulse throughout the ventricular myocardium. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Rank the following from fastest to slowest normal intrinsic discharge rate: SA node, A-V node, Purkinje fibers.","choices":["Purkinje fibers > A-V node > SA node","A-V node > SA node > Purkinje fibers","SA node > A-V node > Purkinje fibers","All three discharge at identical rates"],"answer":2,"exp":"From fastest to slowest intrinsic discharge rate: SA node (70-80/min) > A-V node (40-60/min) > Purkinje fibers (15-40/min) — this hierarchy is why the SA node normally sets the pace, but lower structures can take over (as an \"escape\" rhythm) if the SA node fails. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, in which direction does conduction occur through the A-V bundles?","choices":["Only from ventricles to atria","Normally one-way, from atria to ventricles","Bidirectionally with equal ease in both directions","The A-V bundles do not conduct impulses at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normally, conduction through the A-V bundles is one-way, from the atria to the ventricles, ensuring that the atria always contract before the ventricles under physiological conditions. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Besides increasing heart rate, what other cardiac effects does sympathetic stimulation via norepinephrine produce?","choices":["No measurable effect on contractility, conduction, or heart rate at physiological doses","Increased rate of conduction and increased contractility in atria and ventricles","Decreased cardiac output despite an increase in heart rate and contractility","Decreased contractility with unchanged conduction velocity throughout the atria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation via norepinephrine increases sinus node discharge (rate), increases the rate of conduction of the impulse, and increases the force of contraction in both the atria and ventricles. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the \"funny current\" referred to in SA node phase 4 depolarization?","choices":["A current with no known ionic basis, thought to arise instead from mechanical stretch of the pacemaker cell membrane","An outward potassium current that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 repolarization of pacemaker cells","An outward chloride current that only occurs in ventricular muscle during the plateau phase","An inward Na+ current through special channels that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 depolarization of pacemaker cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"funny current\" is an inward Na+ current through special channels that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 depolarization characteristic of the SA node and other pacemaker tissue, gradually bringing the membrane potential toward threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is \"ventricular escape,\" in the context of strong vagal stimulation?","choices":["A state of complete, permanent cardiac arrest that occurs whenever vagal stimulation exceeds a critical threshold of intensity","An increase in SA node discharge rate that occurs as a reflexive compensatory response to strong, sustained vagal stimulation","A rhythm where, if strong vagal stimulation temporarily stops SA/AV conduction, a lower pacemaker (e.g., Purkinje fibers) takes over to maintain some ventricular contraction","A dangerous, unpredictable arrhythmia that arises spontaneously and has no relationship whatsoever to vagal or sympathetic stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"If strong vagal (parasympathetic) stimulation temporarily halts impulse transmission from the SA/AV node, a lower intrinsic pacemaker (such as the Purkinje fibers) can take over and maintain some ventricular contraction — this is called ventricular escape. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How does the speed of atrial depolarization compare to ventricular depolarization, and what is the functional consequence for the atrial T wave?","choices":["Atrial depolarization is faster than ventricular depolarization, so the first area to depolarize is the last to repolarize, producing a positive wave in leads I, II, and III","Atrial and ventricular depolarization occur at identical speeds, so their respective repolarization waves appear as mirror images of one another on the EKG tracing","Atrial depolarization does not produce any detectable repolarization wave at all, since the atrial muscle mass is simply too small to generate a measurable one","Atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization, so the first atrial area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize, producing what would be a negative wave in leads I, II, and III"],"answer":3,"exp":"Atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization; because of this, the first atrial area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize (unlike the ventricles, where the sequence reverses), which would produce a negative deflection in leads I, II, and III if it weren't obscured by the QRS complex. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"According to the vasodilator theory of local blood flow control, which substances act as vasodilators released by metabolically active tissue?","choices":["Norepinephrine, epinephrine, angiotensin II, and vasopressin acting on smooth muscle","Adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, ADP compounds, histamine, K+ ions, and H+ ions","Albumin, globulin, fibrinogen, and other large circulating plasma proteins","Oxygen, glucose, and free fatty acids delivered directly to arteriolar smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasodilator theory proposes that metabolically active tissue releases vasodilator substances — adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, ADP compounds, histamine, K+ ions, and H+ ions — that increase local blood flow to meet metabolic demand. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What does the oxygen demand theory propose about the relationship between oxygen availability and local blood flow?","choices":["Oxygen availability has no effect on local blood flow, which instead responds only to changes in local CO2 concentration","Only CO2, not oxygen, affects local blood flow, providing the primary acute feedback signal for tissue perfusion","Reduced oxygen availability decreases blood flow further, worsening tissue hypoxia through a positive feedback loop","Reduced oxygen availability increases blood flow, providing acute local feedback control matching flow to oxygen need"],"answer":3,"exp":"The oxygen demand theory proposes that reduced oxygen availability to a tissue increases local blood flow, providing acute feedback control that helps match blood flow delivery to the tissue's oxygen needs. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is autoregulation of blood flow?","choices":["The ability of a tissue to maintain blood flow relatively constant over a wide range of arterial pressures","A phenomenon that only occurs in the heart and kidneys, absent from skeletal muscle and skin circulation","A mechanism requiring direct cortical control, mediated entirely through descending sympathetic pathways","The inability of any tissue to control its own blood flow independent of central nervous system regulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Autoregulation is the ability of a tissue to maintain blood flow relatively constant over a wide range of arterial pressures, explained by both metabolic theory (pressure drop → reduced O2/nutrient delivery → vasodilator release) and myogenic theory (arterioles intrinsically dilate in response to decreased wall tension). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"According to Laplace's law, how is the wall tension of a hollow structure (like a blood vessel) related to internal pressure, radius, and wall thickness?","choices":["Wall tension is inversely related to internal pressure and radius, and directly related to wall thickness","Wall tension is directly related to internal pressure and radius, and inversely related to wall thickness","Wall tension is unrelated to any of these variables","Wall tension only depends on wall thickness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Laplace's law states that the wall tension of a hollow structure (vessel, ventricle, alveolus) is directly related to internal pressure and radius, and inversely related to wall thickness — thicker walls reduce wall tension for a given pressure and radius. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does long-term regulation of blood flow occur, compared to acute mechanisms?","choices":["Long-term regulation does not really exist as a distinct process; only acute vasodilator and myogenic mechanisms actually operate to control tissue blood flow","By changing heart rate exclusively, with absolutely no accompanying change in the size or number of vessels supplying a given tissue","By instantaneous changes in vessel diameter only, occurring within seconds and requiring no structural vascular remodeling whatsoever over time","By changing the degree of vascularity of tissues (size and number of vessels), with oxygen as an important stimulus; long-term mechanisms are more effective than acute ones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Long-term regulation of blood flow occurs by changing the degree of vascularity of tissues (altering the size and number of vessels present), with oxygen serving as an important stimulus for this vascular remodeling; long-term mechanisms are more effective than acute regulatory mechanisms. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is angiogenesis, and what triggers it?","choices":["A process exclusive to the developing fetus, which ceases entirely and permanently once vascular development is complete at birth","A process with no known trigger, occurring randomly and unpredictably throughout all tissues regardless of metabolic demand","The destruction of existing blood vessels, triggered specifically by prolonged exposure to high oxygen tension within the tissue","The growth of new blood vessels, occurring in response to angiogenic factors released from ischemic tissue, rapidly growing tissue, or tissue with high metabolic rates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Angiogenesis is the growth of new blood vessels, occurring in response to angiogenic factors (such as VEGF, FGF, and angiogenin) released from ischemic tissue, rapidly growing tissue, or tissue with high metabolic rates. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which of the following is a humoral (bloodborne) vasoconstrictor agent?","choices":["Histamine","Angiotensin II","Nitric oxide","Bradykinin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Angiotensin II is a potent humoral vasoconstrictor, along with norepinephrine, epinephrine, vasopressin, and endothelin; bradykinin, serotonin, histamine, prostaglandins, and nitric oxide are humoral vasodilators. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Can higher brain centers, such as the hypothalamus, influence the vasomotor center (VMC)?","choices":["Yes — many higher centers of the brain, such as the hypothalamus, can exert powerful excitatory or inhibitory effects on the VMC","Only the cerebellum can influence the VMC, exerting exclusively inhibitory control over its baseline firing rate","Higher brain centers can only inhibit, never excite, the VMC, regardless of the emotional or physiological state involved","No, the VMC operates completely independently of higher brain centers, receiving input only from peripheral baroreceptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Many higher centers of the brain, including the hypothalamus, can exert powerful excitatory or inhibitory effects on the vasomotor center, integrating broader physiological and emotional states into cardiovascular control. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the approximate normal interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, and what causes it?","choices":["About 25 mm Hg, driven by an unusually high interstitial albumin concentration","About 28 mm Hg, identical to plasma colloid osmotic pressure due to unrestricted protein exchange","0 mm Hg, since capillary walls are entirely impermeable to any plasma protein","About 8 mm Hg, caused by an interstitial protein concentration of approximately 3 g/dL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure is normally about 8 mm Hg, resulting from an interstitial protein concentration of approximately 3 g/dL — lower than plasma's protein concentration but still exerting a meaningful osmotic pull that promotes filtration. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What two factors make up the filtration coefficient (Kf) used to calculate filtration rate?","choices":["Blood viscosity and the length of the capillary segment","Plasma protein concentration and net interstitial fluid pressure","Cardiac output and mean systemic arterial pressure","Surface area and the hydraulic conductivity of the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The filtration coefficient (Kf) is the product of the capillary surface area and the hydraulic conductivity of the membrane; filtration rate = net filtration pressure (NFP) multiplied by Kf. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does an increase in hematocrit affect blood viscosity and vascular resistance?","choices":["Increased hematocrit decreases blood viscosity slightly, lowering overall vascular resistance","Increased hematocrit only affects oxygen-carrying capacity, not vessel resistance at all","Increased hematocrit increases blood viscosity, which increases vascular resistance","Hematocrit has no relationship to blood viscosity, which depends only on plasma protein levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"An increase in hematocrit increases blood viscosity, which in turn increases vascular resistance (consistent with Poiseuille's law, where flow is inversely proportional to viscosity). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Approximately how many capillaries are estimated to exist in the body, and what total surface area do they provide for exchange?","choices":["About 100 million capillaries, with a surface area of roughly 50 square meters","Capillary number and surface area vary too much to ever be reasonably estimated","Over 10 billion capillaries, with a surface area of 500-700 square meters","About 1 billion capillaries, with a surface area of 100 square meters"],"answer":2,"exp":"The microcirculation contains over 10 billion capillaries, providing a total surface area of 500-700 square meters for the transport of nutrients and removal of waste products via solute and fluid exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How quickly can the vasomotor center (VMC), via the nervous system, increase arterial pressure?","choices":["Nervous control raises arterial pressure only gradually, over several hours, through slow catecholamine accumulation","Within seconds, by constricting arterioles, constricting large vessels, and directly increasing heart rate/contractility","Only over a period of several hours, by slowly increasing renal sodium retention and blood volume","Over several days, primarily through gradual remodeling of arteriolar smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nervous system, via the vasomotor center, can increase arterial pressure within seconds by constricting almost all arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large vessels (increasing venous return/cardiac output), and directly increasing cardiac output via increased heart rate and contractility — explaining rapid pressure increases during exercise or fright. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is one everyday function the baroreceptor reflex serves related to posture?","choices":["It only functions during exercise and has no role in everyday postural blood pressure changes","It helps maintain relatively constant arterial pressure despite changes in body posture (e.g., standing up from lying down)","It has no relationship to posture changes, which are instead corrected entirely by the renin-angiotensin system","It causes a brief, exaggerated rise in blood pressure whenever a person changes from standing to lying down"],"answer":1,"exp":"The baroreceptor reflex helps maintain relatively constant arterial pressure despite changes in body posture (such as standing up), rapidly correcting the transient pressure drop that gravity would otherwise cause. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How do baroreceptors affect daily variations in arterial pressure, despite adapting over the long term?","choices":["They actively amplify moment-to-moment pressure swings by positively reinforcing whatever direction pressure is already moving, substantially increasing daily blood pressure variability","They oppose either increases or decreases in arterial pressure moment-to-moment, thereby reducing daily variations in pressure, even though they adapt and are unimportant for true long-term (weeks-to-months) control","They only oppose increases in pressure and never decreases, since vagal center activation is physiologically incapable of falling below its resting baseline level","They have no effect on daily pressure variation at all, since baroreceptors are only capable of responding to sustained changes that last several weeks or months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors oppose either increases or decreases in arterial pressure as they occur throughout the day, reducing daily variations in pressure — even though, because they adapt/reset over sustained periods, they are considered unimportant for true long-term (weeks-to-months) blood pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What type of nerve ending are baroreceptors, and in which specific arterial locations are they found?","choices":["Spray-type nerve endings found in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and in the walls of the aortic arch","Free nerve endings found only within the walls of the pulmonary artery, right atrium, and vena cava","Encapsulated Pacinian corpuscles found only within the walls of the ascending aorta and the coronary sinus","Chemoreceptor cells found exclusively within the carotid and aortic bodies, not the sinus wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Baroreceptors are spray-type nerve endings located in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and in the walls of the aortic arch, distinct from the nearby chemoreceptor cells of the carotid and aortic bodies. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"During which phase of the cardiac cycle is coronary perfusion of the heart muscle greatest?","choices":["Diastole, when the myocardium is relaxed and no longer compressing the coronary vessels","Systole, when the open aortic valve allows maximal pressure into the coronary ostia","Only during isovolumic relaxation, briefly, between diastole and systole","Coronary perfusion is greatest during systole, when intraventricular pressure peaks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Coronary perfusion is greatest during diastole, when the relaxed myocardium no longer compresses the coronary vessels running through it; during systole, muscle contraction obstructs coronary flow. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the coronary circulation, do local metabolic factors or neural (sympathetic) influences dominate the regulation of blood flow?","choices":["Only neural sympathetic influences regulate coronary flow; local metabolic byproducts play no meaningful role","Metabolic factors override neural influences on coronary flow, just as they do in skeletal muscle","Neither metabolic nor neural factors influence coronary flow, which instead is set entirely by vessel diameter","Neural influences always dominate coronary flow, unlike the situation seen in skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the coronary circulation, just as in skeletal muscle, metabolic factors override neural influences in determining blood flow — even though sympathetic stimulation can increase cardiac activity (and thus metabolite production) and directly affect vessel tone via adrenergic receptors. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does an intact renin-angiotensin system affect the drop in arterial pressure during severe hemorrhage, compared to when the system is blocked?","choices":["The RAS has no measurable effect on hemorrhage-induced pressure drops, since baroreceptor reflexes alone fully restore pressure to 50 mm Hg","An intact RAS actually accelerates the initial pressure drop by triggering a rapid renal sodium and water loss during the acute phase of a severe hemorrhage","An intact RAS helps buffer the drop in arterial pressure; if the RAS is blocked, arterial pressure does not recover as well after a pressure drop (e.g., to 50 mm Hg from severe hemorrhage)","Blocking the RAS has essentially no effect on pressure recovery, since aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex compensates fully and independently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Severe hemorrhage can lower arterial pressure to as low as 50 mm Hg; when the renin-angiotensin system is intact, it helps buffer this drop and supports pressure recovery, but if the RAS is blocked, arterial pressure does not increase much further, showing the RAS's protective role during acute hypotension. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does increased sodium intake influence extracellular fluid volume (ECFV)?","choices":["Increased Na intake stimulates drinking and ADH secretion (via increased Na concentration/thirst), and changes in Na intake lead to changes in ECFV, since ECFV is determined by the balance of Na intake and output","Sodium intake has no relationship whatsoever to ECFV, since the extracellular fluid volume is regulated exclusively by aldosterone-independent renal water handling mechanisms","ECFV is determined solely by dietary potassium intake and its renal excretion, entirely independent of sodium balance or plasma osmolarity fluctuations at all","Increased sodium intake always decreases ECFV by triggering an osmotic diuresis that substantially exceeds any accompanying water retention response in the kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"As sodium intake increases, it stimulates drinking and (via increased plasma Na concentration) stimulates thirst and ADH secretion; because ECFV is determined by the balance of Na intake and output, changes in Na intake directly lead to changes in extracellular fluid volume. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Do different arterial pressure control mechanisms (nervous, RAAS, renal-fluid volume) act over the same timescale?","choices":["Yes — all mechanisms (nervous, hormonal, and renal) reach maximal effect within seconds and sustain identical potency indefinitely thereafter","Only the hormonal RAAS system has any meaningful timing characteristics; nervous and renal mechanisms act with equal, instantaneous potency","No — different mechanisms operate with varying potency over different time intervals, from rapid nervous reflexes (seconds) to the renal-body fluid mechanism, which dominates long-term control","The renal-body fluid mechanism actually acts the fastest of all, within seconds, while the nervous baroreceptor reflexes take days to weeks to become fully effective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Different arterial pressure control mechanisms operate with varying potency across different time intervals — nervous reflexes (like the baroreceptor reflex) act within seconds, hormonal mechanisms (like RAAS) act over minutes to hours, and the renal-body fluid mechanism dominates true long-term (days to weeks) pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does skeletal muscle blood flow show a rhythmical pattern during rhythmic contraction (e.g., walking)?","choices":["Flow only increases during relaxation and remains fixed at a constant level throughout every contraction phase","Rhythmical flow patterns occur only in smooth muscle organs like the gut, never in skeletal muscle","Vessels are compressed during contraction (reducing flow), so flow rhythmically varies with the contraction-relaxation cycle","Blood flow rises continuously throughout exercise with no rhythmical variation tied to the contraction cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rhythmical contraction of skeletal muscle causes rhythmical blood flow because the vessels running through the muscle are compressed during contraction (reducing flow) and reopen during relaxation (restoring flow). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What happens to previously inactive (\"dormant\") skeletal muscle capillaries during exercise?","choices":["They constrict further during exercise, reducing flow to the resting fibers nearby","They open only during rest and close completely once exercise begins","They open during exercise, increasing nutrient delivery to the active tissue","Dormant capillaries exist only within resting cardiac muscle tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"At rest, some skeletal muscle capillaries have little or no blood flow (\"dormant\"); during exercise, these dormant capillaries open, increasing the capillary surface area available for nutrient delivery to the active muscle. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is notable about sympathetic vasodilator nerves in skeletal muscle blood flow control?","choices":["They have no role in skeletal muscle circulation and are found exclusively in the cutaneous vasculature of the limbs","They are activated by feed-forward stimulation in some animal species, providing anticipatory vasodilation before exercise even begins","They only cause vasoconstriction via alpha receptors, with no anticipatory or feed-forward function in any species","They are the dominant mechanism of exercise hyperemia in humans, overriding local metabolic vasodilation entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic vasodilator nerves, activated by \"feed-forward\" stimulation in some animal species, can anticipate the onset of exercise and begin dilating skeletal muscle vessels even before local metabolic factors from muscle activity take over as the dominant control mechanism. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"From what brainstem nucleus do parasympathetic fibers controlling the ciliary muscle and pupillary sphincter originate?","choices":["The suprachiasmatic nucleus, synapsing directly in the retina","The nucleus tractus solitarius, synapsing in the pterygopalatine ganglion","The Edinger-Westphal nucleus, synapsing in the ciliary ganglion","The superior cervical ganglion, synapsing near the internal carotid artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers controlling eye function arise from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus (associated with CN III, the oculomotor nerve) and synapse with postganglionic fibers in the ciliary ganglion, which then control the ciliary muscle (lens focusing) and the sphincter of the iris (pupil constriction). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the eye originate, and what do the postganglionic fibers innervate?","choices":["They originate in the intermediolateral horn of the first thoracic spinal segment, synapse in the superior cervical ganglion, and innervate the radial fibers of the iris (which open the pupil) plus several extraocular muscles","They originate instead in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, synapse within the ciliary ganglion, and innervate the circular fibers of the iris along with the ciliary muscle","They originate instead within the pterygopalatine ganglion and innervate only the lacrimal gland itself, with no connection whatsoever to iris tone or to extraocular muscle function at all","Sympathetic fibers have essentially no role in eye innervation at all, since both pupil dilation and constriction are mediated entirely by parasympathetic pathways alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the eye originate in the intermediolateral horn of the first thoracic spinal cord segment and synapse with postganglionic fibers in the superior cervical ganglion; these postganglionic fibers innervate the radial fibers of the iris (opening the pupil, mydriasis) plus several extraocular muscles. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What effect does increased light on the retina have on pupil diameter, and what nerve/nuclei mediate this?","choices":["Light itself has no direct effect on pupil diameter at all, which instead varies only with the degree of lens accommodation used for near objects","Pupillary constriction is mediated entirely by the sympathetic nervous system, acting directly upon the radial fibres of the iris via alpha-adrenergic receptors","Increased light causes pupillary dilation via sympathetic stimulation of the radial iris muscle, triggered through the superior cervical ganglion","Increased light causes pupillary constriction (miosis) via parasympathetic stimulation of the pupillary sphincter, triggered by impulses passing from the optic nerve to the pretectal nuclei"],"answer":3,"exp":"Increased light entering the eye causes pupillary constriction (miosis) via parasympathetic excitation of the pupillary sphincter muscle, triggered by impulses passing from the optic nerve to the pretectal nuclei (the pupillary light reflex) — helping the eyes adapt to rapid changes in light conditions. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How does chromatic aberration provide a cue for the accommodation control mechanism?","choices":["Red and blue light focus at exactly the same point in perfectly emmetropic eyes, providing no useful cue except in cases of astigmatism","Only infrared light contributes in any meaningful way to accommodation cues, since it consistently focuses furthest in front of the retinal surface","Red light rays focus posteriorly compared to blue light rays; the eye can detect which color is better focused and use this information to help drive the accommodation response","Chromatic aberration has no relationship to accommodation, which is driven solely by convergence signals from the extraocular muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because of chromatic aberration, red light rays focus slightly posterior to blue light rays; the eye can detect which color appears in better focus and relay this information to help drive the accommodation mechanism toward the correct lens adjustment. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between binocular and stereoscopic vision?","choices":["They are identical terms describing the fusion of retinal images that occurs within the lateral geniculate nucleus","Binocular vision means seeing an object with both eyes simultaneously; stereoscopic vision means seeing objects as three-dimensional","Binocular vision refers to perceiving depth through motion parallax; stereoscopic vision refers to peripheral field width","Neither term relates to eye function; both instead describe auditory localization of sound sources"],"answer":1,"exp":"Binocular vision refers to seeing an object with both eyes simultaneously, while stereoscopic vision refers to perceiving objects as three-dimensional — made possible because slightly different images from each eye are fused in the brain into a single 3-D image. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does eye placement differ between predatory animals (e.g., hawks, lions) and animals that are preyed upon (e.g., rabbits), and what functional tradeoff does this represent?","choices":["Both predatory and prey animals have independently evolved identical eye placement and identical visual field width, since binocular overlap is thought to confer no meaningful survival advantage to either group","Eye placement has no functional consequence for either group at all, since visual acuity and color perception matter far more for hunting success and predator detection than field-of-view geometry","Predators have eyes on the sides of the head for a wide field of view useful in tracking multiple prey animals at once; prey species instead have forward-facing eyes that sacrifice peripheral vision for stereoscopic depth judgment","Predators have forward-facing eyes providing good binocular/stereoscopic vision (useful for judging distance to prey); prey animals have eyes on the sides of the head giving a wide field of view (good for detecting movement/threats) but poor stereoscopic vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Predatory animals (hawks, lions) have forward-facing eyes, giving good binocular and stereoscopic vision useful for judging distance to prey; animals that are preyed upon (rabbits) have eyes on the sides of the head, giving a wide field of view good for detecting movement/threats but providing poor stereoscopic vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Approximately what fixed visual angle do human eyes have, and how does close eye spacing contribute to distance judgment?","choices":["104 degrees, with overlapping visual fields from closely spaced eyes allowing accurate judgment of distance","About 200 degrees, though widely spaced eyes give the two visual fields almost no overlap","About 30 degrees, with eyes spaced so closely that the two fields overlap almost completely","The visual angle is fixed near 60 degrees, entirely independent of interpupillary spacing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Humans have a fixed visual angle of about 104 degrees; because the eyes are close together, their visual fields overlap substantially, which allows for accurate judgment of distance via binocular/stereoscopic cues. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does eye convergence relate to the accommodation (lens-focusing) mechanism?","choices":["Convergence and accommodation are entirely unrelated processes, controlled by completely separate and non-overlapping cranial nerve pathways","The neural signal for convergence causes a simultaneous signal that weakens the lens, decreasing its overall refractive power","Convergence only occurs several seconds after accommodation is fully complete, with the two processes never overlapping in time","The neural signal for convergence (turning the eyes inward for near objects) causes a simultaneous signal that strengthens the lens (increases accommodation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The neural signal driving eye convergence (turning both eyes inward to view a near object) also triggers a simultaneous signal that strengthens the lens, coupling convergence and accommodation together for near vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Does the degree of lens accommodation stay perfectly fixed once an image is in focus?","choices":["Oscillation only occurs when viewing extremely close objects, at a slow rate of roughly once every ten seconds","Yes — once an image has come into focus the lens shape locks permanently until the convergence angle changes substantially","Accommodation only oscillates in young children, stopping completely by around age 20 as the lens stiffens","No — oscillation of the degree of accommodation occurs continuously (about twice per second), helping the system detect and maintain the best focus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The degree of accommodation oscillates continuously (about twice per second) even when an image appears to be in focus; this oscillation helps the visual system detect whether an image becomes better or worse focused as lens thickness changes, guiding fine-tuning of accommodation. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How might the fovea's position within a depression of the retina contribute to accommodation control?","choices":["The fovea only provides fine visual acuity information, entirely unrelated to accommodation or lens focus","The fovea sits at the same depth as the surrounding retina, so no depth-based cue can be extracted from its position","It has no relationship to accommodation control, which instead relies solely on chromatic aberration cues","Differences between the foveal image and the image of the surrounding retina may give clues about which direction the lens should adjust"],"answer":3,"exp":"Because the fovea lies in a slight depression of the retina, differences between the image focused on the fovea and the image on the surrounding retina may provide additional clues about which direction (thicker or thinner) the lens should adjust during accommodation. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Approximately how many different functional types of retinal ganglion cells exist, and what kinds of stimulus features can different types respond to?","choices":["Only 2 distinct types exist, one responding exclusively to light onset and the other exclusively to light offset","More than 20 different types, responding to specific directions of motion/orientation, fine detail, increases/decreases in light, or particular colors","Exactly 3 distinct types exist, corresponding directly one-to-one with the 3 subtypes of bipolar cells feeding into them","Ganglion cells are functionally identical with no diversity at all, since every bit of specialisation occurs earlier at the photoreceptor level"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are more than 20 different functional types of retinal ganglion cells, with different types responding to specific directions of motion or orientation, fine detail, increases or decreases in light, or particular colors — encoding many different aspects of the visual scene before signals even leave the eye. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Is the mechanism by which the brain assembles a coherent conscious visual perception from parallel processing pathways fully understood?","choices":["Only motion perception remains unexplained; both the color and depth pathways are now considered completely mapped and understood","Visual perception requires no neural processing beyond the LGN, with the thalamus alone generating full conscious awareness","No — how the brain actually perceives a visual image is not well understood, despite knowing that three parallel pathways process motion, depth/form, and color","Yes — the exact neural mechanism of visual perception was fully mapped out once the three parallel processing pathways had been identified"],"answer":2,"exp":"How the brain actually perceives a coherent visual image is not well understood, even though it is known that visual perception involves three parallel pathways processing motion, depth and form, and color — visual perception is described as a \"creative process\" that goes beyond simple signal relay. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
